## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about lightweight on-device features under real-world constraints, including energy budgets, privacy regulations, and user consent requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Cost-benefit analysis under resource constraints
- On-device caching strategies and cache invalidation
- Energy-aware prefetching policies
- Privacy compliance and consent overhead
- Phased rollout strategies and success metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
const PREFETCH_CONFIG = {
  batteryThreshold: 0.25,
  network: 'wifi',
  cacheBudgetMB: 75,
  maxMemoryPercent: 15,
  retryIntervalMs: 30 * 60 * 1000
};

class AssetPrefetcher {
  constructor(config = PREFETCH_CONFIG) {
    this.config = config;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }

  async shouldPrefetch(deviceState) {
    const { batteryLevel, networkType, isCharging } = deviceState;
    return (
      batteryLevel > this.config.batteryThreshold &&
      (networkType === 'wifi' || isCharging)
    );
  }

  async prefetch(assets) {
    if (!(await this.shouldPrefetch(getDeviceState()))) return;
    for (const asset of assets) {
      const cached = await caches.match(asset.url);
      if (!cached) {
        const response = await fetch(asset.url);
        await caches.put(asset.url, response.clone());
      }
    }
  }

  async getAsset(url) {
    const cached = await caches.match(url);
    if (cached) return cached;
    return this.handleMiss(url);
  }

  async handleMiss(url) {
    return fetch(url).catch(() => getOfflineFallback(url));
  }
}
```

